t=Topic('8.2')
for l,rows in {
'01':['GDP per capita is GDP divided by population, an average output measure rather than each person’s actual income.','Real income adjusts for prices, while disposable income concerns resources after direct taxes and including relevant transfers.','An aggregate real-income increase differs from an increase in real income per person because population can change.','Non-income indicators such as health, education, housing and safe-water access contribute evidence about living standards beyond output averages.'],
'02':['Living-standard disparities can arise through unequal access to employment, assets, skills, infrastructure and public services.','Similar real output per person can coexist with different income distributions and service access within or between countries.','Development labels and national averages do not remove differences between households or regions.'],
'03':['Information technology can improve access to learning, information, communication and services when households have usable affordable access and skills.','Unequal connectivity, device costs, power reliability and digital skills can limit or unevenly distribute IT benefits.','Privacy risks, misleading information and displacement of some tasks can offset IT benefits depending on use and support.']}.items():
 for row in rows:t.claim(l,row)
t.block('01','plain_explanation','Use output averages alongside other indicators',"""
Living standards concern the goods, services and conditions people can enjoy. GDP per capita divides GDP by population, giving an average output measure. For an invented economy with real GDP N$120 million and 10,000 residents, real GDP per capita is N$12,000. It is not a statement that each resident receives N$12,000 cash or consumes that amount. The distribution and the relation between output and household resources need separate evidence.

Real and disposable describe different adjustments. Real income allows for prices and purchasing power. Disposable income concerns resources after direct taxes and including relevant transfers. An invented N$5,000 money income less N$500 direct tax plus N$200 transfer leaves N$4,700 disposable income. Its purchasing power depends on prices. The calculation has not removed inflation merely by subtracting tax.

Per-capita adds another distinction. If total real income rises 10% while population rises 20%, real income per person falls. Population can change through natural increase or net migration; either can affect the denominator. A larger total is therefore insufficient evidence of a higher average. Even a rising average can leave some households worse off if gains are concentrated.

Non-income indicators help complete the picture. Education access, life expectancy, adequate housing and safe-water access reveal aspects not established by money output alone. They also have limits: an enrolment count does not fully describe learning quality, and a national water-access share can conceal local shortages. Compare the same definitions and periods and consider distribution, environmental conditions and service quality. Several consistent indicators support a stronger conclusion than a single total.
""")
t.block('02','plain_explanation','Explain disparities through access and opportunities',"""
Differences within a country can arise because people have different access to jobs, skills, assets and services. A household near reliable transport and relevant training may have better opportunities than one facing costly journeys and interrupted schooling. Ownership of productive assets can also generate income. These mechanisms explain opportunities and constraints without assuming that a low income reflects lack of effort.

Between countries, productive capital, infrastructure, skills, institutions and market access can differ. So can prices and the quality of services obtained from a given income. A comparison of converted money totals alone can be misleading if the same amount buys different bundles. Use consistent real or purchasing-power information where supplied rather than inventing a conversion.

Consider two invented districts with the same real output per person. In A, gains are concentrated and remote households lack reliable water and schooling. In B, income is more broadly shared and services are accessible. Equal averages do not establish equal lived conditions. The evidence supports a stronger broad-access case for B, while the precise experience of any household still needs its own facts.

A separate simple income illustration makes the average’s limitation visible. Two households receiving N$1,000 and N$9,000 have average income N$5,000. Two receiving N$5,000 each have the same average. The lower-income household’s ability to meet a given essential bill is very different in the first distribution. This is a household-income example, not a claim that GDP is handed out as income. The point is that an average alone cannot reveal how resources are shared or whether the least-resourced group can access essentials.

Developed and developing labels do not erase such variation. Poverty and service gaps can exist within a high-average-income economy, while some households in a lower-average economy have strong access. A judgement should state which group and dimension is being compared. Higher real output per capita can help fund improvement, but distribution and environmental costs condition the living-standard result. Topic 8.1 discusses development and demographic change; the focus here is assessing the resulting disparities.
""")
t.block('03','evaluation','IT can improve access, with an unequal reach',"""
Information technology can help households obtain learning materials, compare information, communicate and use services remotely. An accessible reliable connection may reduce travel time for a routine service or connect a learner with relevant resources. The living-standard mechanism is the useful access and time saved, rather than simply owning a device. Topic 5.2 addresses the distinct output-per-input effect at work.

Suppose an invented village learning hub provides a reliable connection, suitable devices and assistance at an affordable cost. Learners can access materials they previously travelled far to obtain. That can improve opportunity if the content is useful and learners can use it. If another group lacks power, devices or skills, the same online provision may leave it behind and widen access differences.

Costs and risks matter. Data charges can absorb scarce household funds; misleading information can lead to poor decisions; inadequate privacy can expose personal information. Some changes in work processes may displace tasks before workers can adapt. These risks require assessment of use and support before a conclusion about net benefit. Maintaining an accessible alternative route can also matter for households unable to use the digital channel.

The conditional conclusion is that affordable usable access, reliable infrastructure, relevant skills and trustworthy services strengthen the benefits. Broad provision can reduce disparities when it reaches underserved households. Concentrated access can instead give the already well-connected a further advantage. Compare who gains, what useful activity changes and what cost is borne before concluding that a technology rollout raises living standards generally.
""")
def s(n):return next(x for x in t.wo['item_slots'] if x['slot'].split('-')[-2]==f'{n:03}')
def c(n,l,p,a,g):t.card(s(n),l,p,a,g)
c(1,'01','Explain the distinction between GDP per capita and an individual’s actual income.','GDP per capita is output divided by population, an average. An individual’s income depends on the actual distribution and sources of receipts, so it need not equal the average.',['Difference (1): output/population average. Contrast/test (1): individual distribution can differ.'])
c(2,'01','Calculate real GDP per capita from invented real GDP N$120 million and population 10,000.','Real GDP per capita = real GDP / population = N$120,000,000 / 10,000 = N$12,000 per person.',['Formula/substitution (1): N$120m/10,000. Result/units (1): N$12,000 per person.'])
c(3,'01','Analyse a living-standard comparison with equal real GDP per person in two invented districts, but A has concentrated income and limited water access while B has broad income distribution and reliable water access.','The average output measure is equal but access and distribution differ. B has stronger evidence of broadly shared material conditions; equality of GDP per person does not prove equal living standards or every individual’s position.',['Pattern (1): equal averages, different conditions. Evidence (1): distribution/water contrast. Explanation (2): averages conceal access (1), individual and other indicators remain relevant (1).'])
c(8,'01','Explain the error in claiming disposable income is already real income because direct taxes have been subtracted.','Disposable income adjusts for tax and relevant transfers; real income adjusts for prices. Subtracting tax alone does not establish purchasing power.',['Confusion (1): tax adjustment equated with price adjustment. Boundary/test (1): prices needed for real measure.'])
c(9,'01','Explain why a 10% total real-income increase does not establish higher real income per capita if population rises 20%.','Population grows faster than real income, so the amount per person falls. Aggregate and per-capita real measures have different denominators.',['Confusion (1): total increase treated as per-person increase. Boundary/test (1): population grows faster, reducing ratio.'])
c(4,'02','Explain how unequal access to training and transport can contribute to income disparities where genuine jobs require the skill and reliable travel.','Those with access can gain relevant skills and reach vacancies. Those without access face barriers even where they want work, leading to unequal earnings opportunities under the stated job conditions.',['Starting change (1): unequal access. Mechanism (2): skill eligibility (1), reachable jobs (1). Consequence (1): unequal earnings opportunities.'])
c(5,'02','Explain why a high-average-income country can still contain households with poor living conditions when income and services are unevenly distributed.','The average can be raised by high-income groups while others lack resources or services. The country label does not establish each household’s access.',['Context (1): unequal distribution/services. Reason/conclusion (1): average masks household differences.'])
c(6,'03','Explain a living-standard benefit from a village learning hub with affordable devices, reliable connectivity, relevant materials and assistance.','Usable access lets learners obtain resources without the previous long journey. Time and access barriers fall, improving learning opportunity under the stated affordability, reliability and skill conditions.',['Starting change (1): usable hub access. Mechanism (2): materials reachable (1), travel barrier reduced (1). Consequence (1): better opportunity conditional on effective use.'])
c(7,'03','Explain why putting a service online can widen disparities if only already advantaged households have affordable connectivity and digital skills.','Connected households gain convenient access while excluded households cannot use the new route. Without alternative access or support, the benefits are uneven and the gap can widen.',['Context (1): unequal usable access. Reason/consequence (1): gains concentrated while others excluded.'])
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][0],'01 03 02','Define GDP per capita (2). Identify two non-income living-standard indicators (2). Explain a benefit of affordable usable online learning materials replacing a long journey (2). Identify two distributional differences in this invented comparison: A income concentrated and water access limited; B income broadly shared and water access reliable (2).','GDP per capita is GDP divided by population, an average output measure. Education and safe-water access are indicators. Useful online materials can reduce travel and improve learning access when usable. B has broader income distribution and better water access than A.',['Meaning (2): GDP/population (1), average output rather than actual individual income (1). Separate points for indicators (2): education (1), safe water (1). Explanation (2): travel barrier reduced (1), useful learning access (1). Identification (2): distribution contrast (1), water contrast (1). Marks allocation 8.'])
base='Invented districts each have real GDP N$120m and population 10,000. A has concentrated income and limited water/school access; B has broadly shared income and reliable water/school access. Both receive online learning materials, but only B currently has affordable devices, reliable power/connectivity and assistance. Prices and definitions are consistent; no individual cash income is specified.'
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][1],'01 02 03',base+' Describe three comparison features with case details (6). Explain the per-capita calculation and the conditions for IT to improve useful household access (7). Discuss whether the equal averages establish equal living standards (7).','Both have real GDP per capita N$12,000, but income distribution and service access differ. Dividing N$120m by 10,000 gives the average, not individual income. B’s devices, reliable connection and assistance allow materials to be used, reducing travel barriers. A’s excluded households may not gain. Equality of averages is insufficient; broader services and distribution favour B on the supplied dimensions, though other welfare evidence and individual variation remain relevant.',['Data description (6): equal real average (1), N$12,000 detail (1); income pattern (1), concentrated/broad detail (1); service difference (1), water/school detail (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): divide real GDP/population (1), N$12,000 result (1), not actual individual income (1); devices affordable (1), reliable power/connectivity (1), assistance/skills (1), useful materials reduce access barrier (1). Both sides/judgement (7): equal output average supplies one similarity (1), may indicate potential resources (1); distribution differs (1), service/IT access differs (1); condition other welfare facts and individual variation (1); judgement equality not established (1), B stronger broad-access evidence (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'],[base])
cases='Invented economies with the same real GDP per person. Case 1: income highly concentrated, remote households lack reliable water and schooling, IT access favours already affluent groups. Case 2: income more broadly shared and reliable water, schooling and usable IT are widely accessible. No pollution, leisure or individual-health data are supplied.'
t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][2],'02',cases+' Evaluate the claim that the average demonstrates high living standards throughout Case 1 (7). Evaluate the strength and limits of the broad living-standard case in Case 2 (7).','Case 1: the real average is useful about output resources, but concentration and service gaps prevent an all-household inference; IT access can reinforce the divide. Case 2: broad income and services support a stronger shared material-access case, but averages and access figures do not reveal all individual outcomes, environmental costs or leisure. The supplied dimensions favour Case 2 while a complete welfare comparison remains conditional.',['Case 1 marks (7): argument for output-average relevance (1), potential resources (1); argument against concentration (1), service/IT exclusion (1); condition household and other welfare evidence needed (1); judgement throughout claim unsupported (1), tied to distribution/access (1). Case 2 allocation (7): argument for broad resources (1), services/IT access (1); argument against individual variation (1), missing environmental/leisure evidence (1); condition dimensions and coverage stated (1); judgement stronger shared material case (1), not complete welfare proof (1). Total 14.'],[cases])
